## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design comprehensive testing strategies for distributed systems. Multi-region ride-hailing APIs involve complex interactions between services, making end-to-end testing critical for reliability and user experience.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end testing across microservices
- Latency SLIs, error budgets, and SLOs
- Circuit breakers and exponential backoff retries
- Chaos testing and graceful degradation strategies
- Contract testing with OpenAPI specifications and service mocks
- Cross-region data consistency and eventual consistency patterns

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';

export let options = { 
  vus: 100, 
  duration: '30s',
  thresholds: {
    http_req_duration: ['p(95)<500'],
    http_req_failed: ['rate<0.01']
  }
};

export default function() {
  let payload = {
    rider_id: 'user_123',
    pickup: { lat: 40.7128, lng: -74.0060 },
    destination: { lat: 40.7589, lng: -73.9851 }
  };

  let r = http.post('https://rides.example.com/v1/rides', 
    JSON.stringify(payload), 
    { headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'} }
  );

  check(r, {
    'status is 201': (r) => r.status === 201,
    'response time < 500ms': (r) => r.timings.duration < 500,
    'ride ID returned': (r) => JSON.parse(r.body).ride_id
  });

  sleep(1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes end-to-end testing of a distributed transaction orchestrator, focusing on idempotency, compensation, and eventual consistency—critical in payments-like flows.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys and deduplication
- Distributed orchestration and compensating actions
- Partial-failure handling and rollback paths
- End-to-end vs contract tests with downstream services
- Observability and deterministic test data

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test skeleton for Node.js (mocha)
describe('POST /payments idempotent flow', () => {
  it('preserves a single transaction for repeated calls with same idempotency key', async () => {
    const key = 'idem-123';
    await postPayment({amount: 100}, key);
    await postPayment({amount: 100}, key);
    const txs = await listPaymentsByKey(key);
    assert.strictEqual(txs.length, 1);
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate a bank service outage during the transaction and verify idempotence?
- How would you validate that compensation runs only when partial failure occurs?

## Why This Is Asked

Real-time, asynchronous, event-driven integrations with external partners are inherently error-prone. This question evaluates understanding of contract testing, schema evolution strategy, latency SLAs, idempotency, and resilience patterns—all critical for payment infrastructure reliability.

## Key Concepts

- Consumer-driven contracts (Pact) for multi-party API agreements
- Backward-compatible schema evolution with feature-flagged payloads
- Async webhook reliability: retries, dead-letter queues, circuit breakers
- End-to-end latency measurement with OpenTelemetry distributed tracing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pact contract for webhook payload versioning
const { Pact } = require('@pact-foundation/pact');

const provider = new Pact({
  consumer: 'fraud-gateway',
  provider: 'partner-webhook',
  cors: true,
});

describe('Partner Webhook Contract', () => {
  before(() => provider.setup());
  after(() => provider.finalize());

  it('accepts v2 schema with optional risk fields', async () => {
    await provider.addInteraction({
      state: 'partner supports v2 schema',
      uponReceiving: 'a fraud score webhook',
      withRequest: {
        method: 'POST',
        path: '/partners/123/webhook',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: {
          transactionId: 'txn_abc123',
          riskScore: 85,
          newField: 'feature-flag-value', // v2-only field
        },
      },
      willRespondWith: {
        status: 200,
        body: { received: true },
      },
    });
  });
});
```

